    "report_markdown": "# Research report\n**Question:** According to the attached paper, what are the main components of the Transformer architecture?\n**Domain:** General enterprise research \u00b7 **Status:** completed\n\n## Verified findings\n- The Transformer architecture includes an encoder, which is composed of a stack of 6 identical layers (C1). [C1]\n- Each encoder layer in the Transformer architecture has two sub-layers: a multi-head self-attention mechanism and a simple feed-forward layer (C2). [C2]\n- In the Transformer architecture, the attention mechanism allows every position in the decoder to attend over all positions in the input sequence (C3). [C3]\n- Positional information in the Transformer architecture is commonly represented using absolute sinusoidal position encodings (C4). [C4]\n- The use of positional encodings in the Transformer architecture helps mitigate the model's ignorance of position due to the lack of recurrence or convolution (C5). [C5]\n- A residual connection is applied to the outputs of both the attention and feed-forward layers in the Transformer architecture (C7). [C7]\n- The Encoder only configuration of the Transformer architecture is typically used for classification or sequence labeling tasks (C8). [C8]\n\n## Evidence\n| Claim | Status | Source | Source quality | Origin | Quote |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| C1 | supported | AttentionAllYouNeed.pdf, p. 3 | Unverified (0.35) | new | \"The encoder is composed of a stack of N = 6 identical layers.\" |\n| C2 | supported | AttentionAllYouNeed.pdf, p. 3 | Unverified (0.35) | new | \"Each layer has two sub-layers. The first is a multi-head self-attention mechanism, and the second is a simple, position-\" |\n| C2 | supported | MultiModel_Transformaers.pdf, p. 5 | Unverified (0.35) | new | \"The output of the multi-head attention sub-layer will go through the position-wise Feed-Forward Network (FFN)\" |\n| C3 | supported | AttentionAllYouNeed.pdf, p. 5 | Unverified (0.35) | new | \"This allows every position in the decoder to attend over all positions in the input sequence.\" |\n| C4 | supported | Trasformer_Survey.pdf, p. 20 | Unverified (0.35) | new | \"In vanilla Transformer [137], positional information is encoded as absolute sinusoidal position encodings.\" |\n| C4 | supported | Trasformer_Survey.pdf, p. 20 | Unverified (0.35) | new | \"encoded as absolute sinusoidal position encodings.\" |\n| C4 | supported | Trasformer_Survey.pdf, p. 21 | Unverified (0.35) | new | \"Transformer-XL [24] use a sinusoidal encoding to represent positional relationships\" |\n| C5 | supported | Trasformer_Survey.pdf, p. 4 | Unverified (0.35) | new | \"Since Transformer doesn\u2019t introduce recurrence or convolution, it is ignorant of positional information (especially for the encoder). Thus additional positional representation\" |\n| C7 | supported | Trasformer_Application.pdf, p. 5 | Unverified (0.35) | new | \"the Residual Connection is applied to every output of both the attention and feed-forward layers, as illustrated in Figure 2.\" |\n| C8 | supported | Trasformer_Survey.pdf, p. 4 | Unverified (0.35) | new | \"This is usually used for classification or sequence labeling problems.\" |\n| C8 | supported | Trasformer_Survey.pdf, p. 31 | Unverified (0.35) | new | \"A line of work uses the Transformer encoder as its backbone architecture. BERT [28] is a representative PTM that is typically used for natural language understanding tasks.\" |\n\n## Disputed or unverified (excluded from the findings)\n- **C6 \u2014 unresolved:** The Transformer decoder includes additional layers such as Masked Multi-Head Attention compared to the Encoder which does not include this.\n  - Unresolved: not enough accepted evidence (no accepted supporting or contradicting evidence; still insufficient after the final retry round).\n  - rejected supports quote from Trasformer_Application.pdf, p. 5: \"the Decoder also contains Masked Multi-Head Attention layers.\" \u2014 claim_negation_not_supported\n  - rejected supports quote from Trasformer_Application.pdf, p. 5: \"The Decoder module in the transformer architecture is similar to the Encoder module, with the inclusion of additional layers such as Masked Multi-Head Attention.\" \u2014 claim_negation_not_supported\n\n## Verification limitations\n- Claim statuses come from deterministic rules applied to verifier-model judgments: a quote must be verbatim in the cited passage, contain the claim's numbers, not negate it, and cover its key terms. Deeper semantic support still depends on the verifier model.\n- Source-quality tiers are provisional ranking signals from configurable rules, not probabilities, and never prove that a source supports a claim.\n- Findings are limited to the sources searched in this run; absence of evidence is reported as unresolved, not as false.\n\n---\n*Mesh LLM calls 6/12 \u00b7 embeddings 4/4 \u00b7 web searches 0/0 \u00b7 tools 14/30 \u00b7 retry rounds 2/2 \u00b7 56.9 s/300.0 s \u00b7 \u2248 $0.0285 (estimated, INCOMPLETE: some token types have no configured rate)*\n*Findings are limited to the retrieved sources.*"
